{
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "py43-kc-005-01",
      "title": "Range Boundary Check",
      "description": "Write a function that counts how many numbers in a list are strictly less than a given threshold. Remember, the range function in Python excludes the right endpoint.",
      "hints": [
        "Try using a simple loop to iterate through the list.",
        "Consider what happens if the threshold is equal to the first element.",
        "Think about how you would write this using a list comprehension."
      ],
      "sample_io": {
        "input": {
          "numbers": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5],
          "threshold": 3
        },
        "output": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "py43-kc-005-02",
      "title": "Index Off-By-One Error",
      "description": "A student wrote a loop to print elements from index 0 to 5, but the output stopped at index 4. Identify the likely error in the loop condition.",
      "hints": [
        "Recall the syntax for the range function.",
        "What number is missing from the sequence 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5?",
        "Check if the upper bound of the range is inclusive or exclusive."
      ],
      "sample_io": {
        "input": {
          "list": ["a", "b", "c", "d", "e", "f"],
          "start": 0,
          "end": 5
        },
        "output": "a b c d e"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "py43-kc-005-03",
      "title": "List Slicing Logic",
      "description": "Explain why slicing a list with [start:end] does not include the element at index 'end'.",
      "hints": [
        "Visualize the list indices on a number line.",
        "Imagine the slice operation as drawing a box from start to end.",
        "Does the box include the right edge?"
      ],
      "sample_io": {
        "input": {
          "list": [10, 20, 30, 40, 50],
          "start": 1,
          "end": 3
        },
        "output": [20, 30]
      }
    }
  ]
}
